# Every supply whose capacity buffer is sized and which says what it served, beside the most it
# could have served. One row per layer examined, `violates` where the draw is over the line.
#
# A supply cannot serve more than it can make
#   The rule: a supply cannot serve more than its rating plus the headroom it filed. The nameplate
#   is what it committed, the capacity buffer is how far it can run above that, and the draw is
#   what came out. A draw above the nameplate plus the capacity slack is a supply that produced
#   more than it can produce, which is a filing error and never a fact.
#
# The supply's bound, never the demand's
#   "A draw does not exceed what was asked" is the rule this one is not, because a shared line's
#   output is partly somebody else's demand; layers/drawn.sqlc quotes the corpus's instance.
#   Bounding the draw by the demand would fire on a correct document.
#
# Three answers, taken from `pm:Fit`
#   `Fit`'s annotation settles the shape: the whole range clears, the ranges overlap, or the whole
#   range interferes, and the most likely value decides nothing there. A rule deciding on the most
#   likely value alone would decide on the one number the schema rules out for this kind of
#   question.
#
# Only an excess across the whole range is a violation
#   "Did it exceed" and "could it have" are separate answers, and only the first is a violation.
#   The whole draw range above the whole available range is a filing error with no defence. Ranges
#   that overlap are a document that cannot settle its own question, and reporting that as a
#   breach would accuse a filer of something their figures do not say.
#
# The middle answer has nothing to examine on the corpus
#   Few rows examined carry a ranged draw or rating at all, and those that do clear outright.
#   `examples/witnesses` is where the other two answers are exercised.
#
# In one unit, or not at all
#   The draw, the rating and the capacity slack are added and compared, so all three must be in one
#   unit. A row where any differs is left out, and arithmetic/draw_bounded.sqlc names it `not
#   comparable`.
#
# An unmeasured capacity buffer suspends the rule
#   It does not pass it. Some layers do not know how far they can stretch, and taking that as zero
#   would turn "nobody looked" into "there is no headroom" and make up violations out of a gap. A
#   nameplate becomes a ceiling only once somebody says the buffer is empty.
-- pm:Jagged/pm:draw against pm:Nameplate/pm:amount plus pm:Nameplate/pm:capacitySlack.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT d.filing, d.layer,
           -- the whole draw above the whole of what the supply could make
           d.draw_low > d.n_high + d.capacity_high + 1e-9              AS violates,
           CASE WHEN d.draw_low  > d.n_high + d.capacity_high + 1e-9
                THEN format('served [%s, %s] against at most %s: the whole range is over the '
                            'line', d.draw_low, d.draw_high,
                            d.n_high + d.capacity_high)
                WHEN d.draw_high <= d.n_low + d.capacity_low + 1e-9
                THEN format('served [%s, %s] against at least %s: the whole range clears',
                            d.draw_low, d.draw_high, d.n_low + d.capacity_low)
                ELSE format('served [%s, %s] against [%s, %s]: the ranges overlap, so this '
                            'document does not settle whether the supply was overrun',
                            d.draw_low, d.draw_high,
                            d.n_low + d.capacity_low, d.n_high + d.capacity_high)
           END                                                        AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(layers/drawn.sqlc)
    ) d
    WHERE d.capacity_slack IS NOT NULL
      AND d.draw_unit = d.n_unit
      AND d.capacity_unit = d.n_unit
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'draw_exceeds_the_supply'
